3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1-2\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+2\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-140383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(4+3\\text{i}) \\\\ (-1)(4+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4+3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-140383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =3+4\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =3-4\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =4-3\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =4\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(2-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-140384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =2+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =3+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =3-2\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\text{i}}^{2}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}(\\text{i}+1) \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(1+\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ 6-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ -6{\\text{i}}^{2}-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=3\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\lambda =-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =3"] ["math" "\\lambda =3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\lambda =\\text{i}"] ["text" "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\lambda =3\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "1-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}"] ["math" "1+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+2\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-39185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(3+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3+\\text{i}}{5}=-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-140386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-81190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2\\text{i}"] ["math" "3\\text{i}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-81190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-140387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6+3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -15 & -6+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+(-6+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{\\text{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-39187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{29}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-8+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+(8+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}{(1)(1)}=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-39188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "29"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{29}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-39188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "09ebb95b83339dbfa0831c1995ff8acae944db55fa70b607955563a7d9ef075e"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "q-38480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/state.json" "file_sha256" "c02fb2a184e20cf02c82946946eee18fb749d5326291dd318afb7d539469642a"} {"question" "e-5528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/example-5528.json" "file_sha256" "714bf02a7d6d2fdb9136080e23617c31065a1ffd697282f2503a99d6eb5bb681"} {"question" "e-5528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/example-5528.json" "file_sha256" "714bf02a7d6d2fdb9136080e23617c31065a1ffd697282f2503a99d6eb5bb681"} {"question" "e-5527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/6e4a720878567b81cb593aa5a9bfc5aa2c8c7ace61d111bb8934bc94056c04bc.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/example-5527.json" "file_sha256" "6ddc4c583a61fac864f0bfeb8186884195408eccf997a1dda50871fb3ab7563d"} {"question" "e-5527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/0d2e7b9f7011624cf74762419efd784144cf41e2000301d093e1aa612e96f495.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/2e4bc467e9fbc15574d2739766eada3f297500ecf1a69382500271751b2edf65.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/example-5527.json" "file_sha256" "6ddc4c583a61fac864f0bfeb8186884195408eccf997a1dda50871fb3ab7563d"} {"question" "e-5529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/07f01e2a202e29fee28a98d386df1d22fd21efcf1ee4d0a6a4cc56f9b637d57c.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/a547975b3524322daec753a8ddd010e50e815307d72bc07933d1e3674b4b462c.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/53de45d6b1504ec53ca81471731b06ac5524a12230f0414553624c116a6f47c6.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/example-5529.json" "file_sha256" "03d785558516612556fbcaab07220dc6f3dccc02a65d4ee24fe9c575a0bd451f"} {"question" "e-5529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/b212192b142e9b4686a4d39e5957db5f3bfbca6283bfcb1d88a6fc57a4a27848.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/example-5529.json" "file_sha256" "03d785558516612556fbcaab07220dc6f3dccc02a65d4ee24fe9c575a0bd451f"}] "usage" {"q-81184" [] "e-5528" [] "q-38475" [] "q-39185" [] "e-5527" [] "q-38480" [] "q-140387" [] "q-81187" [] "q-81024" [] "q-140385" [] "q-81025" [] "e-5529" []} "basis" 2501 "retractions" [[17592186176397 :question/problem "If $v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ =-2\\textrm{i}$,\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$?"] [17592186175702 :question/problem "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eig