/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/af8f52f23a6fdcd1f070eff5876415cc884ffaafcbb2fe3f8737c31cc4761c86.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186174901 17592186174902 17592186174903 17592186174904 17592186174905 17592186174919 17592186174920 17592186174921 17592186174922 17592186174923 17592186174925 17592186174926 17592186174927 17592186174928 17592186174929 17592186313259 17592186313260 17592186313261 17592186313262 17592186313263 17592186313294 17592186313295 17592186313296 17592186313297 17592186313298 17592186313301 17592186313302 17592186313303 17592186313304 17592186313305 17592186313336 17592186313337 17592186313338 17592186313339 17592186313340 17592186343580 17592186343581 17592186343582 17592186343583 17592186343584 17592186343608 17592186343609 17592186343610 17592186343611 17592186343612] "immutable_fields" [[17592186174900 :answer-field/correct] [17592186174900 :answer-field/id] [17592186174900 :answer-field/key] [17592186174900 :answer-field/type] [17592186174918 :answer-field/correct] [17592186174918 :answer-field/id] [17592186174918 :answer-field/key] [17592186174918 :answer-fi